[[:db/retract 17592186180087 :question/answer-fields 17592186178191] {:db/id 17592186179767 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-6cb153c3-10ae-52e4-a409-49d1a1150376"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179428 :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u,$ and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that we can express the given transformation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v+7 \\\\ u+v+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v \\\\ u+v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformation $\\mathbf T$ maps the endpoints of $\\overline{PQ}$ to the endpoints of $\\overline{P'Q'}.$ So, to find the image of $\\overline{PQ},$ we find the images of each endpoint.\n\nTo quickly carry out this calculation, we set up the following matrices:\n\n- The matrix $U$ containing the endpoints of our segment:\n![](images/8c/8c18a13c717349bccc5af673ee81a9cdc56f9f2a5c32ce75dc8e63fe91765b1e.svg)\n\n- The matrix $B$ that has the same shape as $U$ and with the vector $\\mathbf b$ in each column:\n![](images/16/16c730f4e70bed4d3c8bd9840b457a3cfdad3905b0ada078e71044b73f3f2800.svg)\n\nThe image of $\\mathbf u$ under $\\mathbf T$ is given by $A\\mathbf u + \\mathbf b.$ So, by applying $\\mathbf T$ to $U,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AU+B & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-5 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}7 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -10 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}7 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the endpoints of the image in the $xy$-plane are $(0,-2)$ and $(-3,1).$\n\nDrawing this region in the $xy$-plane, we get the following:\n\n![](images/61/61508357c514f50b27498bee50dce36d22b7f55f0baf8c2d6a58ad2d53dc879d.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179429 :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u,$ and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that we can express the given transformation as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v+1 \\\\ u+v\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v \\\\ u+v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformation $\\mathbf T$ maps the boundary of $\\Delta$ to the boundary of the image. So, to find the image of $\\Delta,$ we find the images of each side.\n\n![](images/69/69d92e24d5b0c81e54d3878793efe7c07d16fc963222997fe4fec4ffb3f93bd5.png)\n\n          \n\nTo quickly carry out this calculation, we set up the following matrices:\n\n- The matrix $U$ containing all vertices of our square:\n![](images/18/1811ded1ce67ec4ebb443816fdf3eb0080b84c6f84852a04b9b66b4e3df35de6.svg)\n\n- The matrix $B$ that has the same shape as $U$ and with the vector $\\mathbf b$ in each column:\n![](images/11/1110f6582a0a398accdf5a7cda1cd9676e79ecc251fa5ef2c080fc19867e0096.svg)\n\nThe image of $\\mathbf u$ under $T$ is given by $A\\mathbf u + \\mathbf b.$ So, by applying $\\mathbf T$ to $U,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AU+B & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertices of the image in the $xy$-plane are $(1,-2), (3,0), (1,2),$ and $(-1,0).$\n\nDrawing this region in the $xy$-plane, we get the following:\n\n![](images/dd/ddd14bdebe21c830396e044721811cf6b9c7bd7db067e4d0ff7d35e02a592894.png)\n\n          \n\nNotice that since $\\det(A) = 2 >    0,$ this transformation is orientation-preserving." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179430 :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $\\mathbf T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx = v - u-6, \\qquad y = v -2u  .\n$$\n\nLet $A$ be the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $\\mathbf T.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ![](images/13/135271f9b5fd7e9883b49d02ab2abcb2ed268081e887892e5c251c27de1e85de.svg)\n\n- $\\det(A) = 1$\n\n- The transformation $\\mathbf T$ is orientation-preserving" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u,$ and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, notice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}v-u-6 \\\\ v-2u\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-u+v \\\\ -2u+v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $\\mathbf T$ is ![](images/dc/dc6ad7fdd5273aa2af71efc5963804edb118fee37596c36a6be3bd30b62467cc.svg)\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n![](images/42/4266dd2d8188293cb54af9bd8431e27b5ec6e5d936f56363fd692b20b0735e74.svg)\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $\\det(A) > 0,$ the affine transformation $\\mathbf T$ is orientation-preserving.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179773 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-914af94d-7042-54e6-acb1-fed694c9a4de"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179434 :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u,$ and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3u+4 \\\\ 4v-2u\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3u \\\\ -2u+4v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $\\mathbf T$ is ![](images/78/783a9503543c1305d982dd65a5f3929398f51db97442606bc03f96484aa03e58.svg)\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n![](images/75/7582ffa6b92e0cba1d8e303231754cfa98a30cfe242d7a6744442462102a02ed.svg)\n\nSo, the area scale factor of the transformation is\n\n$$\n|\\det(A)| = |12| =12.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-6cb153c3-10ae-52e4-a409-49d1a1150376" :question/id #uuid "6cb153c3-10ae-52e4-a409-49d1a1150376" :question/math-academy-id "q-124153" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=4u+v-1,\\,y=-u-3v+1.\n$$\n\nLet $P(-3,1)$ be a point in the $uv$-plane. Suppose that the image of $P$ under the action of $T$ is the point ${P}^{′}$ in the $xy$-plane. What are the coordinates of ${P}^{′}?$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4u+v-1 \\\\ -u-3v+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4u+v \\\\ -u-3v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the image of $P$ under the action of $T,$ we first create a column vector $u$ containing the coordinates of $P:$ \n\n$$\nu=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nApplying $T$ to $u,$ we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of ${P}^{′}$ are $(-12,1).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-69877276-8edf-5389-b9b6-59ddab5b630a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-69877276-8edf-5389-b9b6-59ddab5b630a" :answer-field/id #uuid "69877276-8edf-5389-b9b6-59ddab5b630a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-79732bdc-fd7e-5901-92b4-c5585d62e3b9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-15292884-dc42-57e9-9c54-15240ddfccc9" "answer-99809079-4b95-5960-860d-5696a69fc28e" "answer-73315599-47f7-5acb-b194-b9be8f07b4fe" "answer-79732bdc-fd7e-5901-92b4-c5585d62e3b9" "answer-94a311b2-4197-58fb-9f74-f9df05de251a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-15292884-dc42-57e9-9c54-15240ddfccc9" :answer/id #uuid "15292884-dc42-57e9-9c54-15240ddfccc9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-1,12)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-99809079-4b95-5960-860d-5696a69fc28e" :answer/id #uuid "99809079-4b95-5960-860d-5696a69fc28e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-11,-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-73315599-47f7-5acb-b194-b9be8f07b4fe" :answer/id #uuid "73315599-47f7-5acb-b194-b9be8f07b4fe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-11,0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-79732bdc-fd7e-5901-92b4-c5585d62e3b9" :answer/id #uuid "79732bdc-fd7e-5901-92b4-c5585d62e3b9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-12,1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-94a311b2-4197-58fb-9f74-f9df05de251a" :answer/id #uuid "94a311b2-4197-58fb-9f74-f9df05de251a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1,12)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-914af94d-7042-54e6-acb1-fed694c9a4de" :question/id #uuid "914af94d-7042-54e6-acb1-fed694c9a4de" :question/math-academy-id "q-162181" :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWhat is the area scale factor of the transformation?" :question/worked-solution "The matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}|=1\\cdot 4-3\\cdot 2=-2.\n$$\n\nSo, the area scale factor of the transformation is\n\n$$\n|det(A)|=|-2|=2.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cc6bf158-bc5a-5b83-aecb-e172b7a9b66e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cc6bf158-bc5a-5b83-aecb-e172b7a9b66e" :answer-field/id #uuid "cc6bf158-bc5a-5b83-aecb-e172b7a9b66e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-76b47f26-b551-5ab2-a16b-a5772ef735ad" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f0b0cc75-4660-5f89-b375-205d3cc92d47" "answer-d2847d2c-57b6-59e8-b9ce-cd6f7d5c8fab" "answer-76b47f26-b551-5ab2-a16b-a5772ef735ad" "answer-0bc4fab2-f286-5943-a45f-a20492b01ffd" "answer-ca24dbee-3bf2-5067-b768-9753b1ef80ef"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f0b0cc75-4660-5f89-b375-205d3cc92d47" :answer/id #uuid "f0b0cc75-4660-5f89-b375-205d3cc92d47" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d2847d2c-57b6-59e8-b9ce-cd6f7d5c8fab" :answer/id #uuid "d2847d2c-57b6-59e8-b9ce-cd6f7d5c8fab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-76b47f26-b551-5ab2-a16b-a5772ef735ad" :answer/id #uuid "76b47f26-b551-5ab2-a16b-a5772ef735ad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0bc4fab2-f286-5943-a45f-a20492b01ffd" :answer/id #uuid "0bc4fab2-f286-5943-a45f-a20492b01ffd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ca24dbee-3bf2-5067-b768-9753b1ef80ef" :answer/id #uuid "ca24dbee-3bf2-5067-b768-9753b1ef80ef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186180755 :tutorial/content "Consider the triangle $\\Delta$ with sides $S_1, S_2,$ and $S_3,$  shown below. Let's imagine that we traverse the boundary once in the counterclockwise direction starting from the origin.\n\n![](images/23/231dd5f96cd36d21940fcee894382edc9eabc91b0bf72bb4a8724c8444f76a8c.png)\n\nLet the affine transformation $\\mathbf T$ be defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & ={{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf T,$ we set up the following matrices:\n\n- The matrix $U$ containing all vertices of our triangle, presented in the order shown in the diagram:\n![](images/34/34be6d7cd24938fb72976910cf3ad6a790eb93574915829f621da99f4aec3838.svg)\n\n- The matrix $B$ that has the same shape as $U$ and with the vector $\\mathbf b$ in each column:\n![](images/be/bed30fcc6a1003bee7f2f7888af9c301314b2aba351497db35cf2ee37710c0a1.svg)\n\nThe image of $\\mathbf u$ under $T$ is given by $A\\mathbf u + \\mathbf b.$ So, by applying $\\mathbf T$ to $U,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AU+B & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vertices of the image are given by the columns of the above matrix. Drawing these vertices in the order shown, i.e.\n\n$$\n(1,-1) \\to (1,0) \\to (2,-1),\n$$\n\nwe get the following picture:\n\n![](images/d5/d514834e6e9f108f478002416e863cf9c98d00add230c2e9321664ea14e5db0e.png)\n\n          \n\nNotice that the polygon $D$ is traversed in the *clockwise* direction." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186180756 :tutorial/content "An invertible affine transformation is **orientation-preserving** if it preserves the orientation of any polygon in the plane.\n\nTo understand this, let's go back to our triangle $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane and its image $D$ in the $xy$-plane under the action of the affine transformation $\\mathbf T.$ \n\n![](images/b5/b5c33feb218ae0c1c32aa61984d01b5a317ab88f78277e01c20111a2c0c4efe5.png)\n\nThis transformation does *not* preserve orientation because when we traverse $\\Delta$ *counterclockwise*, its image $D$ is traversed *clockwise.*\n\nWe can identify whether an affine transformation is orientation-preserving or not by examining the matrix $A$ of the associated linear map. We have the following theorem:\n\n*Suppose an affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is defined as* \n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,\n$$\n\n*where $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u,$ and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix. Then, $\\mathbf{T}$ preserves orientation if*\n\n$$\n\\det(A) > 0,\n$$\n\n*and does not preserve orientation if*\n\n$$\n\\det(A) < 0.\n$$\n\nWe sometimes refer to cases where $\\det(A) < 0$ as **orientation-reversing.**" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186180760 :tutorial/content "Suppose an affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$  is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u,$ and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nSimilar to the case of linear transformations, the ** area scale factor** of $\\mathbf T$ is given by the determinant of $A.$\n\nWe can identify whether an affine transformation increases or decreases the area of a polygon $\\mathcal P$ by examining the area scale factor:\n\n- If $|\\det(A)|>1,$ the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ will have a *larger* area than $\\mathcal P.$\n\n- If $|\\det(A)|=1,$ the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ will have the *same* area as $\\mathcal P.$\n\n- If $0 < |\\det(A)| < 1,$ the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ will have a *smaller* area than $\\mathcal P.$\n\nNote that if $\\det(A) = 0,$ the image of $\\mathcal P$ is not a polygon. We discuss this case in more detail in a separate lesson.\n\nFor example, consider the affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2,$ from the previous discussion, defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & ={{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n![](images/7c/7c637211f31b2e8e49fb56f6e60573c47bada4fa36b6c6e0fa7474c3e4d106fc.svg)\n\nSo, the area scale factor of the transformation is\n\n$$\n|\\det(A)| = |-1| = 1.\n$$\n\nThus, the area of triangle $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane and its image $D$ under $\\mathbf T$ in the $xy$-plane have the same area. \n\n![](images/b5/b5c33feb218ae0c1c32aa61984d01b5a317ab88f78277e01c20111a2c0c4efe5.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179859 :activity/expected-seconds 960.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
